# An anonymous block closed by `until` is a postcondition loop in the enclosing activation (book §13; the author, Q54;
# OPUS-Q54-TRAILER-20260929-13): the body runs, then the condition, and the loop ends when it holds.  x counts to 3
# (3 turns); y > 0 already holds, yet the body ran once; a `continue` goes to the condition -- z 1 continues and is
# checked, z 2 continues and stops the loop, so c stays 0 (a continue that skipped the condition gave z 4, c 1);
# a `break` at b 2 ends the loop.  Each counter past 50 breaks its loop, so a broken loop ends (a mutant).  The block
# ran once before -- its `until:` was read nowhere (x 1).  Success 7; x or turns 65, once 66, z or c 67, b 68.
# In a method, natively; its walked twin is unit_walk_until_block_method.
fn: peek () int
    int: x 0
    int: turns 0
    ---
        x: x + 1
        turns: turns + 1
        if: x > 50
            break
    until: x >= 3
    int: y 5
    int: once 0
    ---
        once: once + 1
        if: once > 50
            break
    until: y > 0
    int: z 0
    int: c 0
    ---
        z: z + 1
        if: z > 50
            break
        if: z < 4
            continue
        c: c + 1
    until: z >= 2
    int: b 0
    ---
        b: b + 1
        if: b > 50
            break
        if: b = 2
            break
    until: b >= 10
    int: r 7
    if: x != 3 || turns != 3
        r: 65
    if: once != 1
        r: 66
    if: z != 2 || c != 0
        r: 67
    if: b != 2
        r: 68
    return: r
end: peek
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: peek(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
